---
/**
 * foodcraft / TestimonialsBN — "Panday Coffee Works" ON RECORD.
 *
 * ROLE: the social proof a stranger reads before walking in, set as a page of a
 * technical document rather than as cards. There is no card, no fill, no
 * avatar and no radius: the reviews are a RULED LEDGER — a strong ink rule
 * opens the list, and every quote is one three-column row (index · quote ·
 * attribution) closed by a grey hairline. Which is precisely why this file
 * prints nothing the owner did not put on record: a ledger that invents a line
 * is worse than no ledger.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value below is lifted verbatim from
 * the `data-component="TESTIMONIALS"` element of
 * "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html":
 *   section       border-bottom 1px #17181A, grid minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr)
 *                 → the wrapper's .sec primitive, restated NOWHERE here
 *   rail          border-right 1px #C8C7C2, items end / centred, padding 20px 0
 *                 → .rail
 *   rail label    vertical-rl + rotate(180deg), mono 10px / .3em / caps /
 *                 #A8351B, "06 / On record"                     → .rail-t
 *   content cell  padding 52px 34px
 *   h2            800, clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) / 1 / -.03em / caps,
 *                 margin 0 0 32px, #17181A
 *   list          border-top 1px #17181A
 *   row           <figure>, margin 0, grid
 *                 minmax(0,64px) minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,200px), gap 26px,
 *                 padding 26px 0, border-bottom 1px #C8C7C2,
 *                 align-items: baseline
 *   index         mono 11px, #A8351B (no tracking, no caps, weight 400)
 *   quote         <blockquote>, margin 0, 17.5px / 1.68, #17181A
 *   attribution   <figcaption>, mono 10px / .14em / caps, #5A5C60
 * Mapped to the wrapper tokens holding those exact values: #EFEEEA = --paper,
 * #17181A = --ink and, on the two section rules, --rule-strong; #C8C7C2 =
 * --line; #A8351B = --accent; #5A5C60 = --ink-soft; 52px = --rail-w; 0 = --r;
 * Archivo = --sans/--disp; IBM Plex Mono = --mono. NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS
 * BELOW. Every colour the design uses in this section already had a name.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX `pdw-`, chosen for THIS template (the design's own slot ids are
 * `pd-*`). It is deliberately NOT the `tah-` set from TestimonialsBM nor the
 * `rg-` set from TestimonialsBL: an audit had to rename a whole class set that
 * had been copied verbatim from the sibling before it, and two templates
 * sharing a scoped class name makes every future diff read as a shared
 * component that is not one. Only the house STYLE was taken from BM — this doc
 * block's shape, the coercion helpers, the guard discipline.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME NOTE (theme engine, verified in lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts):
 * the section must NOT be class "testi" — buildOverrideCss emits
 * `.trust, .trust .num, .trust .lbl, .testi, .testi p { color: … !important }`
 * and would repaint the quotes with a scheme colour this design never chose.
 * It is "pdw-record". The file emits no button and no `.hero`/`.cta-band`/
 * `.nav-cta`/`.nav-right`/`.btn-primary` class, so no other engine label pin
 * reaches anything here.
 *
 * READS content.testimonials.items ({quote, who, rating}); tolerates the legacy
 * bare array parked at content.testimonials and the legacy author/name
 * spellings for the attribution. The data-field always names the CANONICAL
 * path, so the first sidebar edit migrates a legacy row onto the real key.
 *
 * COERCED AT EVERY READ — THE ROW FIRST, THEN EVERY FIELD. Nothing upstream
 * type-checks either one. astro-builder's normalizeBlock passes a non-object
 * entry straight through, so `items` can hold a null, and reading `.quote` off
 * that null throws at BUILD time and takes the whole page down. So each row is
 * normalised to an object before a single field is read, and a bare STRING row
 * is read as the quote itself — the legacy shape where the row IS the quote —
 * so owner text is never silently dropped. Then the fields: a quote arriving as
 * a number crashes the build on .trim(); one arriving as an object prints
 * "[object Object]" through set:html. str() passes strings and finite numbers
 * and treats everything else as absent. Blank-after-trim counts as absent too,
 * so a whitespace-only `who` cannot draw an empty figcaption holding a third of
 * the row open. normalizeBlock aliases name/author onto `who` only when `who`
 * is null, so a row carrying who:'' keeps its blank; the fallback chain below
 * is `||` and not `??` so that row still prints the name the owner did type.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "testimonials" — a data-field on a path the schema does not own is a
 * dead hook that no sidebar input can focus)
 *   testimonials.headline           — the uppercase Archivo head, set:html so an
 *                                     owner can place this design's second
 *                                     voice — a COLOUR change to rust, never an
 *                                     italic — inside their own sentence
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.quote   — set:html, the 17.5px blockquote
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.who     — the tracked mono caps attribution
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored, and therefore carrying no data-field:
 *   the rail label "06 / On record" — the spine's index, and the number the
 *     design itself prints on this element. The index counts only the
 *     RAIL-BEARING sections, in render order, starting at the HERO: Hero 00,
 *     About 01, Services 02, Why 03, Gallery 04, How 05, Testimonials 06 (the
 *     design continues Credentials 07, Location 08, Area 09, Faq 10, CtaBand
 *     11). Header, Trust and Footer draw no rail in this design and are not
 *     counted, which is why 06 is NOT this section's ordinal in PageBN's
 *     fifteen-import list. It is decoration, never stored, never edited.
 *   the row numeral 01, 02, 03 — the DISPLAY position after blank rows are
 *     dropped, zero-padded, so the column always counts 01,02,03 with no hole
 *     in it. The data-field indexes beside it are the ORIGINAL ones (below).
 *   the reveal delay, capped at 3 because the wrapper only defines delays 1-3,
 *     so a fourth row must not wait forever.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION — the hard rule of this section:
 *   - NO STAR ROW, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. This design draws none: the
 *     row opens with a mono index numeral, and the only mark it gives a review
 *     is the hairline that closes it — which is exactly the "plain rule" a
 *     review with no rating is supposed to get. So testimonials.items.${i}
 *     .rating is deliberately NOT rendered here rather than being given an
 *     element the designer never drew. Inventing a star row would be inventing
 *     a claim on the owner's behalf in a template whose whole grammar is
 *     hairlines. It is listed under DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED below so nobody
 *     reads it as a missing hook; if it is ever wanted, it needs a design
 *     decision first, not a quiet addition.
 *   - NO review count, NO average score, NO platform name, NO badge. None of it
 *     is in the data contract and the design draws none.
 *   - Quote and attribution are guarded independently and every guard sits
 *     OUTSIDE its tag, so an unattributed quote renders as a quote rather than
 *     as an empty caption holding the row's third column open.
 *   - A row with no quote is DROPPED: the row IS the quote, so a name-only row
 *     would draw a numbered hairline attributing nothing to somebody. The row
 *     stays in the sidebar and reappears the instant a quote is typed.
 *
 * SECTION GATE: the whole <section> renders only when at least one row has a
 * real quote. It deliberately does NOT count the headline. A headline can exist
 * here with no rows at all — the sidebar owns testimonials.headline as its own
 * input, and AI extraction can fill it — and a gate counting it would ship an
 * uppercase heading, a strong ink rule and nothing under it, the one thing a
 * proof section must never do. (Precisely: lib/derive-content-defaults.ts emits
 * NO testimonials block — its Derived shape carries hero/about/services/why/
 * how/gallery/faq/area/location/ctaBand and no testimonials — so unlike most
 * blocks on this page the head here is never machine-filled. The gate is
 * body-only regardless, because the failure it prevents is a head with no rows,
 * whoever typed the head.) The list's opening rule and each row's closing
 * hairline therefore only ever exist because a real row exists.
 *
 * ORIGINAL INDEXES: `i` is captured BEFORE blank rows are dropped, so a blank
 * row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2. Worked through: items
 * [a, blank, c] renders two rows; the second is items[2], and a data-field
 * built from the DISPLAY position would name items[1] — the blank row that is
 * still sitting in the sidebar — so typing into c would overwrite it instead.
 * The visible numeral does use the display position, because it is decoration
 * and must read 01, 02, 03 rather than 01, 03, 04.
 *
 * NO AUTHORED VISIBLE STRING comes from this file except the rail index, which
 * the spine defines as derived decoration and which is aria-hidden. There is no
 * star row here, so there is no aria-label either.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   testimonials.tag      — the schema declares an eyebrow, but this design puts
 *                           NO .kick in this section: the rotated rail index
 *                           already occupies that beat, and a second small
 *                           rust-mono label directly above the head would print
 *                           the same idea twice at the same weight.
 *   testimonials.source   — a "Reviews on Google" line is a platform claim
 *                           nothing in the pipeline verifies, and the design
 *                           draws no source line under the head.
 *   testimonials.bigQuote — there is no pull-quote element in this design.
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.rating — see ANTI-FABRICATION above.
 *   No image: the design's row is type only, with no avatar plate — so there is
 *   no .plate, no .ph, no .cap, no role="img", and nothing that would need an
 *   owner-authored accessible name.
 *
 * NO ANCHOR HELPER, and that is not an omission. The design's TESTIMONIALS
 * element contains no <a> at all, so this file emits no href and owns no
 * data-href-field; copying onPage()/PAGE_ANCHORS/ANCHOR_ALIAS in here would be
 * an unused function rather than a safeguard. For the same reason there is no
 * focusable element in this section, so the wrapper's square duo focus ring is
 * never reached from here. The section also takes NO id: the page's ids are
 * menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main and the design gives this section
 * none of them.
 *
 * WHY .h2 IS RESIZED HERE. The wrapper's shared .h2 is clamp(28px,4.4vw,62px);
 * every section headline in this design, including this one, is
 * clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px)/1. The size is therefore restated, and ONLY the size,
 * the leading and the 32px margin — never the family, weight, tracking, caps or
 * colour, which stay on the primitive. Consequence, stated so it is not read as
 * a bug: the wrapper's ≤760 step for .h2 (clamp(26px,7.4vw,40px)) is outranked
 * for font-size by the rule below. That is deliberate. That step RAISES the
 * headline from 28px at 761px to 40px at 760px, and R8's ladder must not grow
 * as the viewport narrows; the design's own clamp already floors at 28px, which
 * is the mobile size. The other two declarations in the wrapper's ≤760 rule,
 * max-width:none and overflow-wrap:anywhere, are untouched by this file and
 * still apply, and the 20ch cap the wrapper puts on a desktop headline is left
 * exactly as the house set it.
 * NOT A LONE FORK, checked before it was written: AboutBN, ServicesBN,
 * GalleryBN, HowBN, CredentialsBN and FaqBN each state the same
 * clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) once, at base, with no font-size of their own in any
 * query — so every BN headline holds one curve and this section is in step with
 * its siblings at every width, not smaller than them. The ≤760 step is a
 * WRAPPER fault affecting any section that renders a bare .h2; it is raised
 * against PageBN, where all fifteen would get the fix, rather than patched from
 * here.
 *
 * WHY .fig IS BORROWED FOR THE NUMERAL: it is already mono, rust and
 * tabular-nums, which is what an index column wants — a "1" and a "0" must
 * occupy the same width down the page. Only its font-weight is pulled back to
 * 400, because the design's numeral inherits the body weight and .fig sets 500.
 *
 * CONTRAST, computed from this design's native palette (sRGB relative
 * luminance), not copied:
 *   quote --ink on --paper 15.30 · numeral --accent on --paper 5.67 ·
 *   attribution --ink-soft on --paper 5.77.
 *   All three are NORMAL text — 17.5px regular, 11px, 10px — so 4.5 is the bar
 *   that applies and none of them may take the 3:1 large-text relaxation. The
 *   HEADLINE is large text (800 weight, 28-50px), and it is --ink on --paper,
 *   the same 15.30 as the quote, so it clears either bar with room to spare.
 *   Cross-checked, not copied: PageBN's own table prints ink/paper 15.30,
 *   accent/paper 5.67 and ink-soft/paper 5.77 — the same three figures this
 *   file recomputed independently. They agree.
 *   --ink-soft and --accent are both repainted by buildOverrideCss, so these
 *   are native values and not a guarantee under every scheme pick.
 *
 * RESPONSIVE: the wrapper owns the shared 760 and 520 pass, including the rail
 * going horizontal. This file owns only its own row grid: three columns to two
 * at 900 (the attribution drops under the quote, staying in the quote's column
 * so it does not sit beneath the index), two to one at 520. Every type size in
 * this file only ever DECREASES as the viewport narrows, at every breakpoint,
 * and there is no button here to hold a nowrap label.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


const test = content.testimonials || {};

/* Coerce at EVERY read — see COERCED AT EVERY READ above. A string passes; a
   finite number passes as its digits; null, undefined, NaN, an array and an
   object are not content and become ''. Nothing downstream calls a string
   method on raw input, so no shape from the pipeline can fail the build. */
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : (typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '');

const headline = str(test.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  quote?: unknown;
  who?: unknown;
  author?: unknown;
  name?: unknown;
};

/* Tolerate both shapes at every read: the canonical object with .items, and the
   legacy bare array parked at content.testimonials. */
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(test.items)
  ? test.items
  : (Array.isArray(test) ? (test as Item[]) : []);

/* Normalise ONCE, carrying the ORIGINAL array index, then drop the rows that
   cannot render an honest line. Gating on `rows` rather than `items` also means
   a block of blank rows hides the ledger instead of ruling three empty lines. */
const rows = items
  .map((raw, i: number) => {
    /* THE ROW IS COERCED BEFORE ANY FIELD IS READ. A null row would throw on
       `.quote` and fail the build; a bare string row is the quote the owner
       typed and is kept, not dropped. */
    const it: Item = raw && typeof raw === 'object' ? (raw as Item) : ({ quote: raw } as Item);
    return {
      /* the quote keeps its own leading/trailing space — only the GUARD is
         trimmed, so a blank-looking quote cannot open a numbered row */
      quote: str(it.quote),
      who: str(it.who).trim() || str(it.author).trim() || str(it.name).trim(),
      i,
    };
  })
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.quote.trim()));

/* The spine's index for this section's place in the render order. Decoration,
   never stored, never editable — see DERIVED above. */
const RAIL_INDEX = 'On record';

/* Display position, zero-padded like the design's 01 / 02 / 03. Never the
   original index: this numeral is decoration and must not count 01, 03, 04. */
const numeral = (n: number): string => (n < 10 ? '0' + String(n) : String(n));
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="sec pdw-record">
    <div class="rail">
      <span class="rail-t" aria-hidden="true">{RAIL_INDEX}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body pdw-body">
      {headline && (
        <h2 class="h2 pdw-h2 reveal" data-field="testimonials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>
      )}
      <div class="pdw-list">
        {rows.map((row, k) => (
          <figure class="pdw-row reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            <span class="fig pdw-n" aria-hidden="true">{numeral(k + 1)}</span>
            <blockquote
              class="pdw-quote"
              data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.quote`}
              set:html={row.quote}
            ></blockquote>
            {row.who && (
              <figcaption class="pdw-who" data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.who`}>{row.who}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── the content cell. The wrapper's shared .sec-body pads 44/34/40; the
        design pads THIS cell 52px 34px, so only the vertical rhythm is
        restated and the horizontal padding is left on the primitive (its
        clamp already caps at the design's 34px). One clamp, no breakpoint
        override, so the ladder cannot invert. */
  .pdw-body {
    padding-top: clamp(30px, 4.6vw, 52px);
    padding-bottom: clamp(30px, 4.6vw, 52px);
  }

  /* ── the head. Size, leading and margin only — see WHY .h2 IS RESIZED HERE.
        Family, weight, tracking, uppercase and colour all stay on the shared
        primitive, and no font-size for this element appears in any media query
        in this file. */
  .pdw-record .pdw-h2 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0 0 32px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* set:html output carries no scope attribute, so an owner's <em> inside the
     headline is only reachable through :global(). This design's second voice is
     a COLOUR change; Archivo has no italic worth using, so font-style is pinned
     back to normal in case a browser or an editor paste italicises it. */
  .pdw-record .pdw-h2 :global(em),
  .pdw-record .pdw-h2 :global(.em) { font-style: normal; color: var(--accent); }

  /* ── the ledger. The list OPENS on the strong ink rule, the same weight as
        the rule under the whole section; the interior divisions are grey. That
        pairing is the design's grammar and the reason --rule-strong exists. */
  .pdw-list { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }

  /* ── one review = one ruled row. <figure> carries a UA side margin, so the
        reset is load-bearing rather than tidying. Baseline alignment is the
        design's: the numeral, the first line of the quote and the attribution
        all sit on one line, which is what makes this read as a document. */
  .pdw-row {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 64px) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 200px);
    gap: 26px;
    padding: 26px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  /* ── the index numeral. .fig already supplies mono, rust and tabular-nums;
        only the weight is pulled back to the design's inherited 400. */
  .pdw-n {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 400;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* ── the quote. <blockquote> carries a 40px UA side margin, so this reset is
        load-bearing too. Archivo at the design's 17.5px — no serif, no italic,
        no quotation glyphs: this template quotes people the way a report does. */
  .pdw-quote {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 17.5px;
    line-height: 1.68;
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* An owner's <em> inside a quote keeps the quote's own ink. The rust emphasis
     is the HEADLINE's voice; recolouring a customer's words rust would read as
     the roastery talking, not the customer. */
  .pdw-quote :global(em),
  .pdw-quote :global(.em) { font-style: normal; color: inherit; }
  /* The wrapper gives every <p> a 1em bottom margin. A quote pasted as one
     paragraph would then hang an empty line under the last line of the row. */
  .pdw-quote :global(p) { margin: 0 0 0.6em; }
  .pdw-quote :global(p:last-child) { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* ── the attribution. Written scoped rather than borrowed from the shared
        .mono (10px / .2em / 500 / --muted): the design's figcaption here is
        10px / .14em / 400 on soft ink, and borrowing would quietly ship a
        different line than the design specifies. */
  .pdw-who {
    min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE. This file owns only its own row grid; the wrapper owns the
        shared 760 / 520 pass and the rail collapse. Every size below is lower
        than the one it replaces, at every step, so nothing grows as the
        viewport narrows. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* Three columns to two. The attribution moves UNDER the quote rather than
       under the numeral — grid-column:2 is what puts it there; auto-placement
       would drop it into the index column and break the ledger's left edge. */
    .pdw-row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 44px) minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: 18px;
      row-gap: 10px;
    }
    .pdw-who { grid-column: 2; }
    .pdw-quote { font-size: 16.5px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* Two columns to one. At 360px the row is a stack of three full-width
       lines, every one of which already carries overflow-wrap:anywhere, so no
       unbroken string can push the page sideways. */
    .pdw-record .pdw-h2 { margin-bottom: 24px; }
    .pdw-row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      padding: 22px 0;
      row-gap: 8px;
    }
    .pdw-who { grid-column: auto; font-size: 9.5px; letter-spacing: 0.12em; }
    .pdw-quote { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.6; }
    .pdw-n { font-size: 10.5px; }
  }
</style>
